Z||000000||Sootah||10-21-2001||11:33 PM||||Threw rod. Have 85/86 SE V6 car that has been totally trashed (the body) sitting in my backyard. As soon as I get the title for it or whatever I am going to remove the engine and use it's block in my busted-ass 85 GT. There is oil still, no water came out on the dipstick. (Don't know if it would anyway) Engine compartment radiator cap is off and inside is rusted, but hopefully that will all just flush out. Filter to TB body hose is missins but the TB is closed. All of the sparkplugs are still in. Oil cap is off.<P>Anyway, what I am getting at is what all should I do to prep this block for entry into my car? I want to get all possible sludge out of the oil system and all that as well as clean up the inside of the cylinders.<P>Thanx <I><B>-Sootah</B></I><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sootah||10-21-2001||11:36 PM||||May as well throw this in here instead of creating another thread.. When I was driving the car home, before the acursed rod ejection, it felt wobbly. Like I was riding on Jello or something. Never checked tire pressure, may be nothing more than that. Anything else it may be?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Neal||10-21-2001||11:49 PM||||As for the engine, i would take off the oil pan and clean that sucker from the inside, then put in some fresh oil.  Try and flush any coolant crusties out of the block with clean water.  And if the engine has been sitting that long why not put on new oil seals at the crank.<P>And the wobbling check your sway bar end links, wheel bearings, steering rack, controll arm bushings and ball joints.  Play in any of those could cause a wobble||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sootah||10-22-2001||12:08 AM||||How much of the engine that has been sitting should I use?||||reg||3
Z||000004||BlackDragon||10-22-2001||12:27 AM||||i think you shold do like i did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> tear both engines into little parts, pick the pretiest ones, and bring them to a machine shop and let them make the parts new  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but then i am a firm beleiver in engine rebuilds, im in the middle of 3 right now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> cavalier 4cyl, toyota 4 cyl, and fiero 4 cyl. unfortunately i think the fiero will be done last  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <BR>pull it out and tear it down  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>or you could just pull the oil pan, make sure everythink inside looks good, clean the pan clean the engine as good as you can, and throw it together. it will run, but not for 200000 miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>good luck<P>------------------<BR>who is General failure? and why is he reading my hard disk?¿?¿?¿<P>big cars may be fast, but i can corner.....<P>BlackDragonsEmail@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sootah||10-22-2001||09:27 PM||||Rebuild is what I would like, especially to rebuild a 3.4 and slam 'er in there.. But seeing as how I emptied the vast majority of my funds into actually getting the car and I have other debts that must be absolved (not many, but they still suck) I will have to go with just a straight up swap.<P>When I have the block out and oil pan off how do I go about cleaning the internals? I really don't think that I should pressure wash the inside of it or anything so what should I do?<P>Also, I've got the whole SE's engine, what parts should I use from it? Other than the obvious, of course.<P>Oh yeah, Bump<sup>1</sup><P>Thanx 4 all the help guys<BR><B><I>-Sootah</B></I><P><B>EDIT</B>: Readin' Ritein' and Rithma.. Oh, wait, Spellin'<p>[This message has been edited by Sootah (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||webbee||10-22-2001||10:01 PM||||Make sure that the engine can be rotated. If the rings have rusted to the cylinder walls you will have to rebuild.<P>Before you take the pan off drain the old oil out. Put the plug back in and fill the block and heads completely with kerosene. Let it sit for a day or two. If you can add vibration to the block this will help to break up and loosen sludge. Drain the kerosene. Take off the pan and valve covers. Clean up all that you can get too. Let the engine set in an open condition for 24 hours to let the kerosene evaporate as much as possible. Clean the h2o passages and put in new freeze plugs. Reassemble the pan & valve covers with new gaskets. Install a cheap oil filter. <P>Swap the engine.<P>When you put new oil in add a can Marvel Mystery oil instead of 1 quart of oil. Run the engine at idle at start up until it gets hot. You can drive it for up to 500 miles. Drain the oil and replace with new oil & filter. Keep your eye on this 2nd oil fill. If it blackens up you will have to do another oil/filter change.<BR>This should clean the interior of the engine.<BR>If you don't like kerosene you can use auto tranny fluid but it is much more expensive.<P>This is a stop gap measure. Rust has developed in the cylinders and rings and it probably will score the walls, if it has set for any length of time. <P>A swap with out a rebuild is doing the work twice but you gotta do what you gotta do.<P>Good luck! ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sootah||10-22-2001||10:27 PM||||How much are new seals and crap going to cost me? Should I use the same intake, head, ect on it? I'd prefer not to have to tear too much of this apart seeing as how I have no frigging idea what I am doing here (only know what haynes tells me) Also, where do I get the kerosene?||||reg||5
Z||000008||Neal||10-22-2001||10:31 PM||||Just to add to webbee's post try not to rotate the engine, untill you clean it and have fresh oil in there.  I would do exactly what webbee said but when you get the pan and valve covers back on put a little oil in the piston bores, through the spark plug holes.  Fill the engine will oil, pull distributor prime oil pump by using a drill with an 8mm socket on the oil pump drive.<BR>Then rotate the engine AND check compression before you do ANY more work.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sootah||10-22-2001||10:53 PM||||So before I try to rotate it I put the Kerosene in and let it sit a couple of days, or what? What do I do to try to rotate it? If compression is still good then I am not thinking that rotating it by hand is even going to be possible.<P>Once more, Thanx.||||reg||4
Z||000010||Neal||10-23-2001||12:28 AM||||rotating, if the engine is good will be easily done by either the harmonic balancer or the flywheel.  With the spark plugs out there will only be compression in the cyl your checking, also air isnt hard to compress.<P>Yep the kerosene will help break loose all the old sludge.||||reg||1
